From: Arbion Halili <a.halili@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:11:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] feat(castore/fs): add a flat, mmapable metadata index
 format
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Serving a store over virtio-fs, the castore filesystem assigns an inode
to every distinct identity it meets and keeps that node's digest, size,
declared attributes and directory listing on its heap. For a large
immutable tree that is a few megabytes, and every daemon serving the
same content builds its own private replica of it.

Add the artifact that lets N daemons share one copy instead: a flat,
explicitly-laid-out, little-endian file they map read-only, so the
metadata lives once in the host page cache rather than N times on the
heap. A fixed header points at a dense per-inode record table, the
mount-root and per-directory child tables (sorted by name for binary
search), a name/byte pool and a deduplicated extended-attribute section.

Reading it deserializes nothing into owned structures: `Index::parse`
validates the whole file once — every offset, count and cross-reference,
treating it as untrusted input so a truncated or corrupt file is a clean
error rather than undefined behaviour — and the accessors then return
small copies of individual fields or `&[u8]` views into the mapping. The
writer is a pure function of its input, laying sections out in a fixed
order and interning the pool and attrs in first-encounter order, so
identical input yields byte-identical output; that determinism is what
lets every consumer of one file agree on every inode by construction.

The mapping is a few lines of `libc` rather than a dependency: the crate
already links it for the filesystem layer, the map is created once and
never changed, and a read-only file mapping is exactly what shares
page-cache pages between processes.

